Pinned guest registers (M9 track, docs/18-pinned-guest-registers.md,
docs/tracks/m9-tcg-aarch64.md). TCG keeps every global in env at every
basic-block boundary and before every op that may fault, and reloads it on
first use after: 41 % of 7-Zip's generated-code samples on the Air sat on
that traffic. A global can now be pinned in a callee-saved host register
for the life of a chain of TBs (tcg_global_pin_i32/i64; the i386 target
pins eip and the eight GPRs, aarch64 offers x20-x28). The register is
authoritative: the prologue loads the pinned globals, the epilogue stores
them, a helper that may read globals gets them stored first (once per TB
unless modified again), one that may write them has them reloaded after,
and the qemu_ld/st slow path stores them before its helper (a fault never
returns). The allocator never lets a pinned register go to another temp,
produces outputs straight into it (a scratch + mov when the constraints
forbid), and the liveness pass keeps a pinned global live in its register
at block ends and may-fault ops instead of syncing it. Because the
callee-saved registers are now the globals', the aarch64 qemu_ld/st slow
path saves and restores the live caller-saved registers itself
(TCG_TARGET_LDST_SAVES_LIVE) so the fast path no longer spills temps
around memory accesses. The system-mode prologue moves from
tcg_init_machine to tcg_exec_realizefn, after the target created its
globals (user mode already emits it after cpu_create).
-accel tcg,pinned-regs=off is the oracle (nothing pinned, upstream's
allocation); QEMU_TCG_OPTS=pinned-regs=off runs the DOS batteries under it.

